# bootcmd_autosd — boots the AutoSD NFS root under either the BSP kernel or
# the rebuilt 6.1.102-autosd kernel, selected entirely by which values are
# setenv'd into kernel_file/dtb_file/selinux_arg (value table below) — one
# variable change is the whole rollback path.
# Template only: fill ${...} site values from x5h-work/HANDOFF.md at the U-Boot
# prompt. The default bootcmd is never modified; run with: run bootcmd_autosd
#
# Variable provenance:
#   - already defined by this board's U-Boot environment (built-ins): serverip.
#   - CONFIRMED ABSENT on this board, so must be supplied explicitly (see
#     below): kernel_addr_r, fdt_addr_r.
#   - operator-supplied, from x5h-work/HANDOFF.md — setenv ALL SIX of these at
#     the prompt BEFORE THE FIRST setenv LINE BELOW (not just before
#     bootargs_autosd): autosd_export_path, bootargs_bsp, board_ip_config,
#     dtb_file, kernel_file, selinux_arg.
#
#   BSP kernel:     kernel_file=Image          dtb_file=<bsp -uio dtb>                     selinux_arg=selinux=0
#   Rebuilt kernel: kernel_file=Image-autosd   dtb_file=r8a78000-ironhide-uio-autosd.dtb   selinux_arg=enforcing=0
#   (selinux_arg, like the others, expands at setenv time inside the
#   double-quoted bootargs_autosd line — set it BEFORE that line. selinux=0
#   stays the emergency fallback on the rebuilt kernel too. This matters
#   again later, not just at first setup: if you change selinux_arg
#   mid-session to step down the fallback chain, `setenv selinux_arg ...`
#   alone does NOT change bootargs_autosd — it already expanded and stays
#   whatever it was. Re-enter the `setenv bootargs_autosd "..."` line below
#   with the new value, THEN `run bootcmd_autosd`.)
#
# kernel_addr_r / fdt_addr_r are CONFIRMED ABSENT on this board: the
# 2026-08-03 board session's own `printenv` showed neither defined, only
# loadaddr. Its own default bootcmd loads to LITERAL addresses, not through
# these variables, so an undefined ${kernel_addr_r} would silently expand to
# nothing and `tftp` would fall back to ${loadaddr} — a wrong-address load
# that looks like a working command. Before the lines below, setenv BOTH
# kernel_addr_r and fdt_addr_r to the literal addresses from this board's
# default `bootcmd` (read them out of the printenv backup taken at the start
# of the session — never guess). A `printenv kernel_addr_r fdt_addr_r` first
# is a fine sanity check, but expect "not defined" on both — that is this
# board's normal state, not a signal that the setenv can be skipped.
#
# Both of the first two lines below expand their ${...} immediately, at
# setenv time, not at run time: autosd_nfsroot is unquoted, and
# bootargs_autosd is double-quoted. autosd_export_path in particular is
# consumed by the FIRST line, not the second -- setting it only after
# autosd_nfsroot has already run still bakes an empty export path into it
# (nfsroot=<ip>:,nfsvers=3), and that string is plausible enough to pass a
# casual read-back. Only bootcmd_autosd (single-quoted) correctly defers its
# expansion to `run` time. After entering all three lines, read back
# `printenv autosd_nfsroot bootargs_autosd` and check that the export path
# and the ip= config are actually present in the output -- not just that the
# strings "look complete" -- before `run bootcmd_autosd`.
#
# The nfsroot options mirror the option string this board's own BSP netboot
# is proven to mount with (nfsvers=3, nothing else). Do not "improve" it to
# vers=3,tcp: proto=tcp is already the kernel's default here (confirmed by
# findmnt on the running BSP: proto=tcp with only nfsvers=3 requested), so
# the extra options buy nothing and diverge from the one string known to
# work on this hardware.
#
# bootcmd_autosd chains with && rather than ';' deliberately, and NOT only
# for taste: these lines are typed onto the console through tmux
# `send-keys -l`, whose argument parser treats a bare ';' argument as a tmux
# command separator, so a semicolon is silently eaten mid-line (see the
# console gotchas in x5h-work/HANDOFF.md). && is also what this board's own
# default bootcmd uses, and it aborts the chain if a tftp fails instead of
# booting whatever happens to be at that address.
#
# The board's saved U-Boot environment may already hold a bootcmd_autosd
# from a previous session, with the kernel filename hardcoded instead of
# ${kernel_file} -- setenv replaces a variable outright, but only when
# re-entered. Skipping straight to `run bootcmd_autosd` on a board that
# already has one saved runs THAT stale command, hardcoded filename and
# all, ignoring whatever kernel_file/dtb_file/selinux_arg were just
# setenv'd. Always re-enter all three setenv lines below first, then
# `printenv bootcmd_autosd` -- not bootargs_autosd, which a stale
# bootcmd_autosd can still produce a plausible-looking one for -- and
# confirm ${kernel_file} appears UNEXPANDED in the output before
# `run bootcmd_autosd`.
setenv autosd_nfsroot ${serverip}:${autosd_export_path}
setenv bootargs_autosd "${bootargs_bsp} root=/dev/nfs rw nfsroot=${autosd_nfsroot},nfsvers=3 ip=${board_ip_config} ${selinux_arg}"
setenv bootcmd_autosd 'tftp ${kernel_addr_r} ${kernel_file} && tftp ${fdt_addr_r} ${dtb_file} && setenv bootargs ${bootargs_autosd} && booti ${kernel_addr_r} - ${fdt_addr_r}'
